1
00:00:01,680 --> 00:00:02,240
Hey everyone.

2
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:06,880
In part one we looked at basic pricing logic for commonly owned substitutes and complements.

3
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:09,440
In this video we're taking things a step further.

4
00:00:09,760 --> 00:00:13,560
We're going to explore how these concepts apply in real business strategy.

5
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:20,040
Perhaps we need to retire a substitute or perhaps our complements can actually power our platform pricing.

6
00:00:20,040 --> 00:00:21,720
So let's go ahead and dive into it.

7
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:24,840
The first thing we want to do is start with substitutes.

8
00:00:24,840 --> 00:00:28,600
Here we are looking at pricing a substitute to avoid cannibalization.

9
00:00:28,840 --> 00:00:35,880
Well, typically that's what we do, but sometimes we may want to cannibalize, that is before somebody else does it for us.

10
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:37,840
Take Apple for example.

11
00:00:37,840 --> 00:00:42,680
When they launched the iPhone, they effectively killed their own ipod line.

12
00:00:43,080 --> 00:00:50,520
But they did it intentionally because they knew smartphones were going to converge everything that is music and Internet phone and the camera.

13
00:00:50,760 --> 00:00:55,800
So Apple sacrificed the ipod to make room for a much more valuable product.

14
00:00:56,280 --> 00:00:58,920
This is called strategic cannibalization.

15
00:00:59,320 --> 00:01:03,000
The logic here is, are you defending against a competitive threat?

16
00:01:03,160 --> 00:01:05,840
Is the current product near the end of its life cycle?

17
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:10,360
Can the new substitute command higher margins or generate more loyalty?

18
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:17,960
If the answer is yes to any of these, then you might want to launch that substitute and go ahead and make that smart long term move.

19
00:01:18,120 --> 00:01:22,120
If you don't cannibalize your own product, someone else will.

20
00:01:22,920 --> 00:01:24,760
Now let's talk about compliments.

21
00:01:25,160 --> 00:01:33,320
Compliments are powerful because you can price one product low or even at a loss and make your own money back on the other good.

22
00:01:33,640 --> 00:01:36,000
Game consoles are a classic example.

23
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:41,520
Microsoft might lose money on every Xbox they sell, but they make up for it in the high margin.

24
00:01:41,520 --> 00:01:43,000
Video games and subscriptions.

25
00:01:43,960 --> 00:01:45,000
Same Gillette here.

26
00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:47,840
They give away or heavily discount the razor handle.

27
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:50,520
But the blades, that's where the profit lives.

28
00:01:51,480 --> 00:01:56,360
This strategy works when the complement is consumable or repeat purchases.

29
00:01:56,920 --> 00:02:01,240
The base product creates lock in and the combined usage drops value.

30
00:02:01,640 --> 00:02:03,560
We also see that with bundling.

31
00:02:03,640 --> 00:02:07,880
So Amazon prime for example, includes music, video streaming, free shipping and more.

32
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:10,640
And they may price each separately.

33
00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:15,280
But if they price them all together, they don't have to sell individual services anymore.

34
00:02:15,280 --> 00:02:16,960
They're now selling a platform.

35
00:02:17,360 --> 00:02:20,320
So in pricing complements, think about product margins.

36
00:02:20,320 --> 00:02:22,720
Think about the ecosystem that you want to create.

37
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:26,520
Now let's look at a numerical example.

38
00:02:26,520 --> 00:02:29,120
And with this, the cross price elasticity of demand.

39
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:34,160
So we know that substitutes and complements are going to have different cross price elasticities.

40
00:02:34,240 --> 00:02:36,800
We're going to start with a substitute numerical good.

41
00:02:37,360 --> 00:02:42,640
We have two Colas, Cola A, Cola B, Cola obviously different because one's red and one's blue.

42
00:02:43,040 --> 00:02:45,680
Now, Cola A is the lower margin product.

43
00:02:45,680 --> 00:02:48,480
Cola B is the higher margin product.

44
00:02:48,960 --> 00:02:51,880
We have Cola a selling for $1.

45
00:02:51,880 --> 00:02:57,440
We sell 1,000 of those and our margin is $0.20 per unit sold.

46
00:02:57,760 --> 00:02:59,840
Cola B sells for A$20.

47
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:04,880
It sells 600 and its margin is $0.50.

48
00:03:05,280 --> 00:03:10,320
So with the old profit or the original profit, what we see is a total profit of $500.

49
00:03:11,120 --> 00:03:20,720
This comes from multiplying the amount sold for Cola A by its margin to get $200 and the amount sold of Cola B by its margin to get $300.

50
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:27,920
But now what we want to do is implement a new pricing strategy based off of what we know of commonly owned substitutes.

51
00:03:28,240 --> 00:03:32,480
Here our price change is to raise Cola A to $1.15.

52
00:03:32,960 --> 00:03:34,960
This gives us a 15% increase.

53
00:03:35,040 --> 00:03:42,840
Remember, we're raising the price on the lower margin item more because this can help push customers to the higher margin item.

54
00:03:43,240 --> 00:03:47,400
And so Cola B is going to have its price rise to $1.25.

55
00:03:47,400 --> 00:03:49,960
This is only 4.2% increase.

56
00:03:50,040 --> 00:03:53,560
And what we see is our sales are going to adjust down because the price increase.

57
00:03:53,960 --> 00:03:58,120
But our sales adjust down to 850 units for Cola A.

58
00:03:58,120 --> 00:04:01,800
And Cola B is now 700 units being sold.

59
00:04:02,440 --> 00:04:07,800
See, we move people from the lower margin good to the higher margin good.

60
00:04:07,960 --> 00:04:13,960
Now our new profit is going to be 8150 units of the Cola A times 35 cents.

61
00:04:13,960 --> 00:04:16,520
And that gives us 297.50.

62
00:04:16,600 --> 00:04:21,960
Now that extra 15 cents comes from that increase in the price of the good.

63
00:04:21,960 --> 00:04:24,600
Our margin increases by the $0.15 change.

64
00:04:24,920 --> 00:04:28,440
Cola B, our profit is now $385.

65
00:04:28,680 --> 00:04:32,920
We had a change of $0.05 in our margin.

66
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:41,160
So we multiply the 55 margin by 700 units sold to get the $385 in profit.

67
00:04:41,160 --> 00:04:45,320
So now our total is $682.50.

68
00:04:45,320 --> 00:04:58,960
And we can see that we have a $182.50 profit gain simply by looking at the items pricing the lower margin item higher than the higher margin item.

69
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:04,640
Now let's move on to our compliments numerical example.

70
00:05:04,720 --> 00:05:08,800
So here we're going to have our smartphone and our subscription plan.

71
00:05:09,640 --> 00:05:13,560
And with these, we are selling this smartphone for $450.

72
00:05:13,560 --> 00:05:20,120
We have 400 units being sold and our margin is $100 per unit.

73
00:05:20,120 --> 00:05:32,040
With the subscription plan, we sell these at $50 per month and we get $20 a month in our margin and that gives us a margin of $240 per year.

74
00:05:32,440 --> 00:05:38,760
Now, our original profit here, we're going to sell the phones 400 times $100 to get us $40,000.

75
00:05:38,760 --> 00:05:44,960
To plan, we sell 400, multiply that by the yearly margin to get $96,000.

76
00:05:45,120 --> 00:05:48,720
So our combined profit is $136,000.

77
00:05:49,120 --> 00:05:52,880
Now, because these are compliments, we're going to lower the price on both of these.

78
00:05:53,600 --> 00:05:56,880
And so the new smartphone price is going to be $425.

79
00:05:57,120 --> 00:05:59,680
The new monthly plan is going to be $47.

80
00:06:00,320 --> 00:06:06,400
And this causes our quantity demand of both of those to increase to 500 units being sold.

81
00:06:07,440 --> 00:06:15,280
So our new profit becomes 500 phones times the $75 margin, and that gives us $37,500.

82
00:06:15,440 --> 00:06:24,720
And recall, the reason why our margin is now 75 as opposed to 100 is because we cut into that margin by lowering price by $25.

83
00:06:25,360 --> 00:06:27,400
The same logic applies to the plan.

84
00:06:27,400 --> 00:06:36,240
We sell 500 plans, we have a $204 margin, which comes from the taking out $3 per month.

85
00:06:36,720 --> 00:06:39,360
So 3 times 12 gives us 36.

86
00:06:39,440 --> 00:06:47,760
So take the $240 margin that we had, subtract out the now $36 that we lose, and that's where the 204 comes from.

87
00:06:47,920 --> 00:06:51,360
So that plan nets us 102,000 per year.

88
00:06:51,600 --> 00:06:54,960
Combined profit is $139,500.

89
00:06:55,360 --> 00:07:00,000
And we can see that the profit gain is going to be $3,500.

90
00:07:01,720 --> 00:07:07,960
So what we see is lowering prices on both complement products, increased overall adoption and lifted profits.

91
00:07:08,280 --> 00:07:13,560
This shows how coordinated price cuts across complements can grow the entire bundle.

92
00:07:13,560 --> 00:07:17,880
Now, these numerical examples here help quantify what cross price elasticity describes.

93
00:07:18,040 --> 00:07:20,040
Substitutes move in opposite directions.

94
00:07:20,040 --> 00:07:21,480
Complements move together.

95
00:07:22,040 --> 00:07:27,400
But with smart pricing across your product line, you can actively shape those outcomes to your advantage.

96
00:07:27,640 --> 00:07:29,280
Now, this is an exercise for you.

97
00:07:29,280 --> 00:07:35,680
Calculate what the cross price elasticity is for the substitute goods and the complement goods that we just did.

98
00:07:36,880 --> 00:07:41,840
Now, I'm going to move on to our mergers, acquisitions and product strategy.

99
00:07:42,240 --> 00:07:46,320
Sometimes the substitute or complement isn't one of your own products.

100
00:07:47,040 --> 00:07:48,480
Sometimes it's someone else's.

101
00:07:48,480 --> 00:07:50,320
And that's where acquisitions come in.

102
00:07:50,640 --> 00:07:53,360
Facebook buying Instagram, well, that's a substitute.

103
00:07:53,360 --> 00:07:55,200
Instagram was competing for attention.

104
00:07:55,440 --> 00:07:58,960
Facebook bought it to neutralize the threat and integrate its value.

105
00:07:59,560 --> 00:08:02,840
Disney buying Pixar and Marvel, well, those are compliments.

106
00:08:03,080 --> 00:08:09,240
These IPs fit perfectly into Disney's flywheel movies, merchandising parks and streaming.

107
00:08:09,560 --> 00:08:14,760
So by owning the compliments, Disney increased the value of its entire ecosystem.

108
00:08:16,120 --> 00:08:18,520
And now let's look at some final tips here.

109
00:08:18,520 --> 00:08:26,680
Managing our portfolio product means asking, how does pricing one product affect everything else that I sell?

110
00:08:26,680 --> 00:08:27,960
So here are some quick tips.

111
00:08:28,600 --> 00:08:30,920
Don't optimize products in isolation.

112
00:08:32,200 --> 00:08:35,080
Use bundling or platform pricing when possible.

113
00:08:36,520 --> 00:08:39,560
Watch for customer overlap across product lines.

114
00:08:39,720 --> 00:08:44,120
And then lastly, think lifetime value, not just per unit profit.

115
00:08:44,440 --> 00:08:47,720
So strategic pricing here isn't just about setting our numbers.

116
00:08:47,720 --> 00:08:51,880
It's about playing offense and defense in our product ecosystem.

117
00:08:51,960 --> 00:08:55,800
When we own the substitutes and complements, we control the chessboard.

118
00:08:56,160 --> 00:08:58,400
So we need to make our moves wisely.

119
00:08:59,040 --> 00:09:00,640
That's it.  Hope you've enjoyed it.